{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYLtnMGD6xZFhH6XK4A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"About a week before the Cursor Marketplace incident, an unknown attacker replaced the release binaries of five already-published versions of OVHcloud's official CLI (ovh/ovhcloud-cli) with secret-stealing malware. The uploads came through the GitHub account of a legitimate OVH contributor, which had been hijacked in March 2026 as part of the GlassWorm operation. The Hacker News mentions it alongside the Cursor case. It does not say the two attacks are linked, and nothing in the article establishes that they are.\n\nRead side by side, the two incidents show different routes to the same place. In the OVH case the attacker altered binaries under version numbers that already existed. In the Cursor case a new version, 2.1.313, appeared under the real anthropic.claude-code ID. Cursor says at most 5,000 users installed it during roughly 11 hours on October 29, and Jenn Gile of OpenSourceMalware noted that anyone with Claude Code set to auto-update could have received it without doing anything. In both cases the code arrived through a channel and under a name the user had already decided to trust.\n\nMy inference from the OVH details is that a version number a team had vetted earlier would not have revealed swapped binaries, though the article does not discuss that. What the article does support is the response Microsoft recommends for the Cursor case: identify every system that installed or auto-updated to 2.1.312 or 2.1.313 from Cursor's marketplace, preserve evidence, isolate the hosts, and rotate credentials. Cursor has still not said how the attacker was able to publish under Anthropic's name."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1541,"cache_read_input_tokens":6361,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens"